            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of both the original house and the front addition are 2x6 construction with an estimated R20 batt insulation. As discussed for your future planning, I have modeled adding an addition R10 breathable exterior rigid insulation. It is recommended to have the exterior rigid insulation represent at least 50% of the R-value of the insulation located within the stud wall.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls up to a minimum R30 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 279&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

CRAWLSPACE WALLS



Your crawlspace is heated via a heat transfer pipe near the upper ceiling that travels down into the crawlspace, using an inline fan to push the heat (from the woodstove) into the crawlspace. There is no insulation in the floor above the crawlspace (which is normal for a heated crawlspace), however there is also no insulation on the concrete perimeter walls, the perimeter of the floor header space above, or on the floor of the crawlspace (all are areas for heat loss). I have modelled adding 5.5 inches (R33) of polyurethane foam insulation to the interior of the crawlspace walls &amp; the perimeter header space above.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 5.5 inches (R33) of polyurethane foam insulation to the crawlspace walls (and header space above), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 541&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;67.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a 40 USG Rheem electric hot water tank, that you have also connected some heat loops to provide some in-floor heat via the crawlspace. As per our discussion, electric hot water (and electrically heating your home) at $0.70/kWh in Fort Simpson (once you exceed the Gov't subsidy threshold) is a very expensive option. We discussed an option for you to use a high efficiency propane boiler to become the primary heating system for your home (still use the woodstove to offset as big a portion of your heating as you like), however you would now have a primary heating system that will allow you to be away from home for a period of time AND this same boiler can heat your domestic hot water (keeping the same water storage tank) much more cost effectively.



Please see additional comments in the Heating System comments section of this report.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The estimated annual savings for this upgrade is in the Heating System comments section of this report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

You are currently heating your house primarily with your woodstove. As a backup, you have established some heat loops from your 40 gallon electric hot water tank that run through the crawlspace. As you had outlined to me (and I have validated with the energy modeling I have done for your house) the electric hot water heating is not only VERY expensive (at $0.70/kWh in Fort Simpson once you exceed the Gov't subsidy threshold), the hot water tank is not large enough to meet the design heat load requirements for your home. So ............ you continue to ensure the woodstove is your primary heating system and you self-harvest wood to keep it cost-effective.



We did talk about an option that you may want to research further. I think you are on the right track with looking at hot water heating &amp; you could install an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler at 95% AFUE. This boiler could be used to establish a few thermostatically controlled heating zones where the heat distribution can be a combination of in-floor heat &amp; use some of the newer "flat panel" wall rads. The multiple thermostats could help you better control the heat for certain zones of the house (main living area, master bedroom, other bedrooms &amp; the main bathroom).



One other feature with the boiler is that you can have one dedicated loop to heat your domestic hot water, and keep the current storage tank to provide a "buffer". The Energy Factor (EF) of the current electric hot water tank is 0.93, and the EF of using the 95% AFUE boiler to also heat your hot water is 0.79. Although a lower energy efficiency that the electric tank ............. the cost differential between electricity &amp; propane in Fort Simpson more than offsets this !!



Thor - I am not saying you need to use the boiler to heat your house 100% of the time. You can still use your woodstove for a very high contribution (offset) to your heating requirements, however the propane bioler would provide your family peace of mind to have a heating system that will allow you to be away from the home for some extended periods of time.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

There are three savings scenarios to consider. For these, we use our most current fuel cost library information for Fort Simpson, and this is included in the "Additional Comments" section of my report. Although you self-harvest wood, we still attach a nominal cost of $100 per cord to cover truck, trailer, chainsaw, gas &amp; oil related expenses.



1. Using the new 95% AFUE ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler to heat your house 100% of the time. This is estimated to cost you an additional &lt;b&gt;$ 2,340&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, however decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;37.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



2. Using this same boiler to also heat your domestic hot water. This is estimated to save you &lt;b&gt;$ 1,282&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. For 1 &amp; 2 combined, the net estimated additional cost is &lt;b&gt;$ 1,058&lt;/b&gt; per year - assuming no wood stove use at all.



3. Taking both 1 &amp; 2 above into consideration, what would be the cost differential to use your woodstove to offset 75% of your annual heating needs. This is expected to save you &lt;b&gt;$ 1,198&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. This is the number you could consider when doing your payback analyses for the high efficiency boiler system that provides you with peace of mind if you need to be away from home for a period of time during the heating season ......... and still allows good savings if you continued to use your wood stove for about 75% of the heating season.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for number 1 above, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the left SE facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 967&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 in a diesel electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has the following ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood is not yet vented to the outside, however this is your plan this year.

2. The master bathroom fan is also not yet vented to the outside, however this is your plan this year.

3. The main bathroom does have an exhaust fan that vents to the outside, and it appears to operate well.

4. The clothes dryer vents to the outside, however it appeared to have less airflow than the bathroom fan. You may want to check in the crawlspace to see if the dryer vent pipe has some accumulated water in it (and sagging) if it is a flexible vent pipe that is being used? 



With 4 full-time occupants in this house, there is a high probability that 3 or even all 4 of these exhaust fans could all be on at the same time, causing enough negative pressure in the house to compete with the wood stove, especially when the door is opened to put more wood on the fire.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (from the list in the section above, this included the main bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer) indicated a pressure drop of 5.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals (and will become even greater once the range hood &amp; master bathroom fan are exhausted to the outside), the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from the woodstove.



During my assessment of your home, there were both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in the house. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air - Wood Stove&lt;/b&gt;



Building on the 2 previous sections, I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the wood stove, so this means the wood stove is drawing from inside heated air for combustion &amp; sending it up the chimney. Having a dedicated combustion air pipe helps to prevent depressurization when other exhaust fans in the house are on, however please remember that depressurization will still occur every time the wood stove door is opened.



I am not sure if your current wood stove can have a dedicated outside combustion air directly connected to it ? If not, and you are one day looking to upgrade/replace this woodstove, there are many new models that do have a direct connection. The purchase of a new eligible woodstove does qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps&lt;/b&gt;



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



